{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"SCOPe interprets these proteins as divergent members of the ferritin-like superfamily (a.25.1), within the ferritin-like fold (a.25) and all-alpha class (a). Ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins, and manganese catalases occupy distinct families within that superfamily. Their shared four-helix-bundle core and related metal-binding architecture support probable common ancestry despite substantial functional divergence. Superfamily membership is an evolutionary inference; shared fold or class alone would not establish homology.

Their chemistries illustrate diversification of this scaffold. Catalytically active ferritins oxidize Fe(II) and support storage as a ferric mineral inside a protein cage. The ribonucleotide-reductase-like family includes class I RNR radical-generating small subunits: their metal-dependent cofactor supplies the radical chemistry needed by the separate catalytic subunit for nucleotide reduction. This does not mean every RNR-like protein is an RNR or uses the same metal/radical cofactor. Manganese catalases use a dimanganese center to disproportionate hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.

Consequently, neither a superfamily assignment nor a conserved metal-binding motif licenses transfer of iron-storage function, RNR activity, catalase activity, metal identity, or oligomeric organization between these families. Even family membership does not guarantee an identical biochemical role. A remote structural template can support modeling of the conserved core, but not automatically its precise active site, cofactor, assembly, or mechanism. Functional assignment requires independent sequence/profile and phylogenetic evidence, examination of catalytic residues and relevant structures, and ideally biochemical activity measurements plus metal/cofactor characterization. Representatives should match the target family and functional subgroup; one ferritin-like structure cannot represent the chemistry of the entire superfamily.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
